---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CleanBox from '../components/CleanBox.jsx';

/**
 * Editorial companion to /recipe-cleaner/: answers the question, then offers the tool.
 * Factual claims are sourced (see docs/search/sources.md); no invented statistics.
 */
const SITE = 'https://www.simpler.recipes';
const pageUrl = `${SITE}/why-recipe-sites-have-long-stories/`;
const title = 'Why do recipe websites have such long stories? · Simpler Recipes';
const description =
  'The real reasons every online recipe opens with an essay: advertising economics, search incentives, copyright, and genuine storytelling — plus a way to skip straight to the recipe.';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      '@id': `${pageUrl}#article`,
      mainEntityOfPage: pageUrl,
      headline: 'Why do recipe websites have such long stories?',
      description,
      author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Simpler Recipes', url: `${SITE}/` },
      publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Simpler Recipes', url: `${SITE}/` },
      datePublished: '2026-09-06',
      dateModified: '2026-09-06',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Simpler Recipes', item: `${SITE}/` },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Why recipe sites have long stories', item: pageUrl },
      ],
    },
  ],
}).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" canonical={pageUrl}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={jsonLd} />
  </Fragment>

  <main id="main-content" class="min-h-screen bg-background">
    <article class="max-w-[680px] mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-10 md:py-16">
      <header class="mb-8">
        <h1 class="font-serif text-[30px] sm:text-[36px] leading-[1.15] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] text-sand-900">
          Why do recipe websites have such long stories?
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-sand-700">
          Short answer: because the story pays for the recipe. Ads, search visibility and copyright all reward
          the essay — and sometimes the writer simply has something to say. If you just want the recipe,
          paste the link here:
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mb-12">
        <CleanBox client:load id="story-clean" examples={true} />
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-5 text-[16.5px] leading-relaxed text-sand-700 [&_h2]:font-serif [&_h2]:text-[22px] [&_h2]:font-medium [&_h2]:text-sand-900 [&_h2]:mt-9 [&_a]:text-sand-800 [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-[3px] [&_a]:decoration-sand-400 hover:[&_a]:decoration-sand-800">
        <p>
          You came for the oven temperature. You met three generations of the family, a road trip through
          Tuscany, and a golden retriever named Biscuit. This isn't an accident, and it isn't vanity — it's
          how the economics of free recipes work.
        </p>

        <h2>The ads pay by the scroll</h2>
        <p>
          Most independent recipe sites earn their living from display advertising, and ad networks pay per
          thousand impressions. A longer page holds more ad slots, and a reader scrolling toward the recipe
          card sails past every one of them. The writing above the recipe is, quite literally, the part of
          the page that pays the writer (<a href="https://bootstrapped.ventures/why-do-recipe-blogs-have-stories/" rel="nofollow">Bootstrapped Ventures</a> has a candid breakdown from the food-blog tooling side).
        </p>

        <h2>Search engines rewarded the essay</h2>
        <p>
          For years, food bloggers watched detailed, wordy pages outrank terse ones, and drew the obvious
          conclusion. Whether or not Google ever required it — it says it doesn't — the belief alone was
          enough to make every pancake recipe open with a treatise on Saturday mornings. Once your
          competitors all write 1,500 words, publishing 150 feels like unilateral disarmament.
        </p>

        <h2>You can't copyright an ingredient list</h2>
        <p>
          Under US copyright law, a bare list of ingredients can't be protected; the
          <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/help/faq/faq-protect.html" rel="nofollow">US Copyright Office</a> notes protection may exist
          "where a recipe or formula is accompanied by substantial literary expression." The story isn't
          just decoration — it's the part of the page the author can actually own.
        </p>

        <h2>And sometimes it's just writing</h2>
        <p>
          Worth saying plainly: many food writers tell stories because they're writers. The context —
          where a dish comes from, why the technique matters, what to do when it goes wrong — is often
          genuinely useful, and the best of it is a pleasure to read. The frustration isn't that stories
          exist; it's that you can't choose when to read them.
        </p>

        <h2>So we made the choice separable</h2>
        <p>
          Simpler Recipes takes a recipe link and gives you back just the recipe: the ingredients (check
          them off, scale them), the steps (with a hands-free Cook Mode), the times, and a clear link to the
          original. Quantities and steps stay exactly as the author wrote them — nothing paraphrased,
          nothing invented — and every cleaned recipe credits its source. Read the story when you're
          browsing; skip it when the pan is already hot.
        </p>
        <p>
          Here's what that looks like on a real recipe:
          <a href="/recipes/chicken-tikka-masala/">Chicken Tikka Masala</a>, cleaned from the original at
          RecipeTin Eats (credited and linked on the page). Or paste any recipe link in the box above.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
